SLIDES_PART2 = [{'id': 9, 'title': 'Nearest Thousands', 'subtitle': 'Rounding to 1,000 and When Rounding to 100 Gives the Same Result', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To round a number to the <strong>nearest thousand</strong>, we check which multiple of 1,000 it is closest to on a number line (e.g., 1,000, 2,000, 3,000, 4,000, etc.). The halfway mark between consecutive thousands is <strong>500</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Rule for Nearest 1,000</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Look at the <em>Hundreds digit</em>:<br>\n                • If Hundreds digit is <strong>0, 1, 2, 3, or 4</strong> (< 500) → Round DOWN to lower thousand.<br>\n                • If Hundreds digit is <strong>5, 6, 7, 8, or 9</strong> (≥ 500) → Round UP to higher thousand.<br>\n                <em>Example:</em> In <strong>2,346</strong>, hundreds digit is 3 (< 5) → rounds to <strong>2,000</strong>.<br>\n                In <strong>7,820</strong>, hundreds digit is 8 (≥ 5) → rounds to <strong>8,000</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-amber-200">\n                <strong>Let Us Think (Fascinating Puzzle):</strong> Can rounding a number to the nearest 100 give the <em>exact same answer</em> as rounding it to the nearest 1,000?<br>\n                <strong>YES!</strong> Take <strong>2,980</strong>:<br>\n                • Rounded to nearest 100 → <strong>3,000</strong> (since 80 ≥ 50, rounds up from 2,900 to 3,000).<br>\n                • Rounded to nearest 1,000 → <strong>3,000</strong> (since 980 ≥ 500, rounds up from 2,000 to 3,000).<br>\n                Both give 3,000!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'When measuring long train journeys or aeroplane flights across India, we round distances to the nearest thousand! To round to the nearest thousand, we inspect the digit in the Hundreds place. If the Hundreds digit is five, six, seven, eight, or nine—meaning five hundred or more—we round up to the next thousand. If it is less than five, we round down. For example, two thousand eight hundred fifty rounds up to three thousand. Now, here is a brilliant mathematical curiosity that will make you think like a true mathematician: can rounding a number to the nearest hundred give the exact same milestone as rounding it to the nearest thousand? Yes, it can! Consider the number two thousand nine hundred eighty. When rounding to the nearest hundred, we look at the Tens digit, which is eight. Since eight is greater than five, two thousand nine hundred eighty rounds up to three thousand! Now round the same number, two thousand nine hundred eighty, to the nearest thousand: we look at the Hundreds digit, which is nine. Since nine is greater than five, it rounds up to three thousand as well! Both roundings land on three thousand! Use the dual-rounding analyzer on your screen to test numbers like four thousand nine hundred sixty and seven thousand nine hundred seventy-five, and explore this wonderful mathematical harmony!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 4,625 rounded to the nearest thousand (1,000)?', 'options': ['4,000', '4,600', '5,000', '4,500'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The Hundreds digit of 4,625 is 6 (which is 5 or more), so 4,625 is closer to 5,000 than to 4,000. It rounds UP to 5,000.', 'study_insight': '625 is greater than the halfway mark of 500, pulling the number up to the next thousand.'}, {'question': 'Which of these numbers gives 4,000 when rounded to the nearest 100 AND also 4,000 when rounded to the nearest 1,000?', 'options': ['3,420', '3,965', '4,450', '4,850'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'For 3,965: Rounded to nearest 100 gives 4,000 (3,965 rounds up from 3,900 to 4,000). Rounded to nearest 1,000 also gives 4,000 (965 ≥ 500).', 'study_insight': 'Numbers near 4,000 with high hundreds digits (like 3,950 to 3,999) round to 4,000 in both hundred and thousand roundings.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Transport Speeds', 'subtitle': 'Comparing Speeds in km/h and Distance = Speed × Time Calculations', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Different vehicles travel at vastly different speeds. Speed tells us <strong>how many kilometres a traveller covers in one hour (km/h)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="overflow-x-auto my-3">\n                <table class="w-full text-center text-xs border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">\n                    <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-amber-400 font-bold">\n                        <tr>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Mode of Transport</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Average Speed</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Distance in 5 Hours</th>\n                        </tr>\n                    </thead>\n                    <tbody class="bg-slate-950/70 text-slate-200">\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Walking on Foot</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-sky-400">4 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">4 × 5 = <strong>20 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Bullock Cart / Camel</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-sky-400">5 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">5 × 5 = <strong>25 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Express Passenger Train</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-emerald-400">80 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">80 × 5 = <strong>400 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Commercial Aeroplane</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-amber-400">800 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">800 × 5 = <strong>4,000 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-medium">Spacecraft in Orbit</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 text-purple-400">28,000 km/h</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-mono">28,000 × 5 = <strong>140,000 km</strong></td>\n                        </tr>\n                    </tbody>\n                </table>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300"><strong>Golden Travel Formula:</strong> <span class="text-amber-300 font-bold font-mono">Distance = Speed × Time</span>. If you know how fast you travel and for how many hours, you can calculate the total distance covered!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Speed tells us how fast an object moves, measured as the distance covered in one hour, written as kilometres per hour or km/h. To calculate the total distance covered during any journey, we use the fundamental mathematical formula: Distance equals Speed multiplied by Time! For example, if a passenger train travels at a speed of eighty kilometres per hour for four hours, the total distance covered is eighty multiplied by four, which equals three hundred twenty kilometres. If you travel on a modern superfast train at one hundred twenty kilometres per hour for five hours, you cover one hundred twenty multiplied by five, which equals six hundred kilometres! Now think about an aeroplane flying at eight hundred kilometres per hour. In just three hours, it covers eight hundred multiplied by three, which equals two thousand four hundred kilometres, flying almost the entire length of India from north to south! And what about an orbital spacecraft flying at twenty-eight thousand kilometres per hour? In two hours, it covers an astounding fifty-six thousand kilometres, circling more than the entire circumference of Earth! Experiment with the interactive flight deck on your screen: select different vehicles, adjust the travel duration slider from one to twelve hours, and watch the distance formula calculate every journey in real time!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A high-speed train travels at a constant speed of 120 km/h. How far will it travel in 3 hours?', 'options': ['240 km', '360 km', '400 km', '480 km'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Distance = Speed × Time = 120 km/h × 3 h = 360 km.', 'study_insight': 'Multiplying the distance covered in 1 hour by the number of hours gives total distance.'}, {'question': 'An aeroplane flies at 850 km/h. How many hours will it take to complete a non-stop flight of 3,400 km?', 'options': ['2 hours', '3 hours', '4 hours', '5 hours'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Time = Distance ÷ Speed = 3,400 ÷ 850 = 4 hours.', 'study_insight': 'Division is the inverse operation of multiplication: 850 × 4 = 3,400.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Trip Planning', 'subtitle': 'Vehicle Capacity Math: Calculating Buses and Vans for School Field Trips', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Planning a school excursion or railway tour requires practical division, multiplication, and remainder management. Let us solve a real-world field trip scenario:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Scenario: Grade 5 Nature Excursion</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A total of <strong>210 students and teachers</strong> are going on a study trip to a wildlife reserve.<br>\n                • A large bus can carry <strong>50 passengers</strong>.<br>\n                • A mini-van can carry <strong>15 passengers</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200 space-y-1">\n                <strong>Step 1:</strong> How many full 50-seater buses can we fill?<br>\n                210 ÷ 50 = <strong>4 full buses</strong> (carrying 4 × 50 = 200 passengers).<br>\n                <strong>Step 2:</strong> Remaining passengers = 210 − 200 = <strong>10 passengers</strong>.<br>\n                <strong>Step 3:</strong> Can 10 passengers fit in one 15-seater mini-van? <strong>Yes!</strong><br>\n                <strong>Optimal Fleet:</strong> 4 Buses and 1 Mini-van (with 5 empty spare seats).\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">If we hired only 50-seater buses, we would need <strong>5 buses</strong> because 4 buses only hold 200, leaving 10 behind!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Planning a school excursion requires sharp mathematical thinking to ensure that every student and teacher has a comfortable seat without wasting money on empty vehicles! Imagine our school is organizing a field trip for one hundred thirty-five travellers. Standard school buses have fifty seats each, while smaller mini-vans have fifteen seats each. How many vehicles should the school hire? Let us divide! First, we divide one hundred thirty-five by fifty. Fifty goes into one hundred thirty-five two times, because fifty multiplied by two equals one hundred. This leaves a remainder of thirty-five passengers. So, the school books two large fifty-seater buses, carrying one hundred passengers. Now, what do we do with the remaining thirty-five passengers? We divide thirty-five by fifteen. Fifteen multiplied by two is thirty, which leaves five students without seats! We cannot leave anyone behind, so we must round up and book three mini-vans, which provide forty-five seats. Two large buses plus three mini-vans provide a total of one hundred forty-five seats, seating all one hundred thirty-five passengers safely with ten extra seats to spare! Slide the headcount slider on your screen from twenty to two hundred fifty passengers to see how mathematics dispatches the perfect fleet for every journey!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A school has 145 students going on a museum visit. If each bus holds 40 students, how many buses are needed so that no student is left behind?', 'options': ['3 buses', '4 buses', '5 buses', '10 buses'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '145 ÷ 40 = 3 with a remainder of 25 students. 3 buses seat only 120 students. To seat all 145 students, a 4th bus is necessary.', 'study_insight': 'In real-world transport problems with remainders, we must round UP to ensure everyone has a seat.'}, {'question': 'If each ticket for a 45-seater bus costs ₹120, what is the total ticket collection for a completely full bus?', 'options': ['₹4,500', '₹5,400', '₹6,000', '₹4,800'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Total collection = 45 seats × ₹120 = ₹5,400.', 'study_insight': '45 × 120 = 45 × 12 × 10 = 540 × 10 = ₹5,400.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Everyday Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Finding 4-Digit and 5-Digit Numbers Around Us in Real Life', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Large numbers are not just written on classroom chalkboards; they exist all around us in daily life, nature, literature, and community planning.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Words in a Book</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A typical children\'s storybook has about 150 words per page. A 100-page novel contains approximately <strong>15,000 words</strong>!</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Trees in an Orchard / Forest</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A community forest reserve with 250 rows of 50 trees each holds 250 × 50 = <strong>12,500 shade and fruit trees</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">School Campus Students</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A large school cluster across 30 sections with 40 students each educates 30 × 40 = <strong>1,200 students</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-purple-400 block mb-1">Town Population Census</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A growing highway junction township has 8,500 households with an average of 4 members each = <strong>34,000 citizens</strong>.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Estimating and multiplying large numbers helps city planners build the right number of water tanks, hospitals, and parks!</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Large five-digit numbers are not just abstract symbols in a math textbook; they are all around us in the real world every single day! Think about the book you are reading right now. A complete Joyful Mathematics textbook contains approximately eighteen thousand four hundred fifty words. That is a five-digit number where one is in Ten Thousands, eight is in Thousands, four is in Hundreds, five is in Tens, and zero is in Ones. Now picture a grand international cricket stadium during a match: there are forty-two thousand five hundred spectators cheering in the stands, which means four ten-thousands, two thousands, and five hundreds! When forest officers protect wildlife reserves, they count twenty-five thousand six hundred eighty trees in a protected sanctuary. And when governments conduct a census, a small bustling town may have sixty-eight thousand nine hundred residents! Understanding five-digit numbers allows engineers to build bridges, doctors to order medicines, and city planners to supply clean water and electricity to thousands of families. Tap on the different real-world scenarios on your screen to inspect their place value breakdowns and see how mathematics measures the vibrant world around us!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A dictionary has 400 pages with an average of 60 words on each page. Approximately how many total words are in the dictionary?', 'options': ['2,400 words', '24,000 words', '240,000 words', '240 words'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Total words = 400 pages × 60 words/page = 24,000 words.', 'study_insight': 'Mental math trick: 4 × 6 = 24, then attach the three zeroes (two from 400, one from 60) to get 24,000.'}, {'question': 'A stadium has 25 rows of seats, and each row has 80 seats. How many spectators can sit in the stadium?', 'options': ['200', '1,500', '2,000', '20,000'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '25 rows × 80 seats = 2,000 seats.', 'study_insight': '25 × 8 = 200; 200 × 10 = 2,000.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'River Crossing', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Classic Boatman, Lion, Sheep, and Grass Puzzle', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">During long train journeys, travellers have enjoyed solving recreational math brainteasers for centuries! One of the most famous river-crossing logic puzzles dates back over 1,200 years:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Dilemma</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">A boatman must ferry a <strong>lion</strong>, a <strong>sheep</strong>, and a <strong>bundle of grass</strong> across a river in a small rowboat.<br>\n                • The boat can hold only the <strong>boatman and ONE item</strong> at a time.<br>\n                • <em>Danger 1:</em> If left alone without the boatman, the lion will eat the sheep!<br>\n                • <em>Danger 2:</em> If left alone without the boatman, the sheep will eat the grass bundle!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>The 7-Step Winning Solution:</strong><br>\n                1. Take the <strong>Sheep</strong> across (leaves Lion and Grass safely together).<br>\n                2. Row back empty.<br>\n                3. Take the <strong>Grass</strong> across.<br>\n                4. Bring the <strong>Sheep BACK</strong> to the start (crucial move! Don\'t leave Sheep with Grass).<br>\n                5. Take the <strong>Lion</strong> across (leaves Sheep alone at start; Lion & Grass are safe together).<br>\n                6. Row back empty.<br>\n                7. Take the <strong>Sheep</strong> across! All three arrive safely across the river!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'During long journeys across our country, travellers historically shared riddles, brainteasers, and puzzles to pass the time and sharpen their wits. One of the most famous puzzles in mathematical folklore is the River Crossing Puzzle! A boatman arrives at the riverbank with a lion, a sheep, and a bundle of lush green grass. He must ferry all three across to the opposite bank. However, his small boat can carry only himself and one item at a time. Furthermore, he faces two strict danger rules: if left alone together on either bank without the boatman, the lion will eat the sheep, and the sheep will eat the grass! How can he transport all three safely? The secret lies in a clever seven-step sequence! First, the boatman takes the sheep across, because the lion does not eat grass. Leaving the sheep on the far bank, he returns alone. Next, he takes the lion across. But to prevent the lion from eating the sheep, he brings the sheep BACK to the starting bank! Then, he leaves the sheep and takes the grass across to join the lion. Finally, he returns empty-handed to fetch the sheep one last time! Play this puzzle on your screen, test your logical deductions, and experience the joy of mathematical problem solving!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In the river crossing puzzle, which item MUST the boatman take across the river on his very first trip?', 'options': ['The Lion', 'The Grass bundle', 'The Sheep', 'He should row across empty'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'He must take the Sheep first. If he takes the Lion, the Sheep eats the Grass. If he takes the Grass, the Lion eats the Sheep. Leaving Lion and Grass together is completely safe!', 'study_insight': 'Identifying compatible pairs (Lion and Grass) is the key insight to unlocking constraint logic puzzles.'}, {'question': "What is the critical 'reverse move' on trip 4 that prevents the sheep from eating the grass on the far bank?", 'options': ['He leaves the boat behind and swims', 'He brings the Sheep back with him to the starting bank', 'He ties the lion to a tree', 'He feeds half the grass to the lion'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'On trip 4, after delivering the grass, the boatman rows the Sheep back to the starting bank. This prevents the sheep from eating the grass on the far side while allowing him to fetch the lion.', 'study_insight': 'In problem solving, moving backward temporarily is sometimes the only path to moving forward successfully.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Pebble Game', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The 2-Player Nim Strategy and Multiples of 4', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When travellers sit together on long journeys, they often play strategy games using simple pebbles, coins, or matchsticks. One classic game is the <strong>Pile of Pebbles Game</strong>:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Game Rules</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">• Start with a pile of pebbles (e.g., <strong>15 pebbles</strong>).<br>\n                • Two players take turns taking <strong>1, 2, or 3 pebbles</strong> from the pile.<br>\n                • The player forced to take the <em>very last pebble loses</em> (or the player who takes the last pebble wins, depending on variant).</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>The Secret Mathematical Strategy: Multiples of 4!</strong><br>\n                Notice that 1 + 3 = 4, and 2 + 2 = 4. Whatever number <em>x</em> the opponent takes, you can always take <strong>4 − x</strong> so that every round removes exactly <strong>4 pebbles</strong>!<br>\n                • If the goal is to take the last pebble: Always leave a multiple of 4 for your opponent (4, 8, 12, 16...).<br>\n                • With 15 pebbles: Player 1 takes <strong>3 pebbles</strong>, leaving 12 (a multiple of 4). Whatever Player 2 takes, Player 1 complements to 4, guaranteeing victory!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Here is another timeless pastime played by travellers under the shade of banyan trees: the Strategic Pile of Pebbles Game! Two players sit beside a pile of fifteen pebbles. On each turn, a player may take one, two, or three pebbles from the pile. The player who picks up the very last pebble wins the game! Can you guarantee a win every single time? Yes, if you understand the mathematics of multiples of four! Notice that whatever number of pebbles your opponent takes—whether one, two, or three—you can always take enough pebbles so that the sum of both turns equals four! For example, if they take one, you take three. If they take two, you take two. If they take three, you take one. In every round, the pile decreases by exactly four pebbles! Since fifteen divided by four equals three with a remainder of three, the winning strategy is clear: on your very first turn, take exactly three pebbles! This leaves twelve pebbles, which is a multiple of four. From that moment on, whenever your opponent takes N pebbles, you take four minus N pebbles, ensuring you leave eight, then four, and finally take the last pebbles to victory! Challenge the computer on your screen and master the multiple-of-four strategy!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In the pebble game, if your opponent takes 2 pebbles on their turn, how many pebbles should you take to make the round total 4?', 'options': ['1 pebble', '2 pebbles', '3 pebbles', '4 pebbles'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'To make the round total 4, you take 4 − 2 = 2 pebbles.', 'study_insight': 'Complementary pairs: (1, 3), (2, 2), and (3, 1) all sum to 4.'}, {'question': 'If you start with 15 pebbles and want to leave a multiple of 4 on the table for your opponent, how many pebbles should you take on your very first turn?', 'options': ['1 pebble (leaving 14)', '2 pebbles (leaving 13)', '3 pebbles (leaving 12, which is 3 × 4)', 'None'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '15 − 3 = 12. 12 is a multiple of 4 (4 × 3 = 12). Leaving a multiple of 4 allows you to control the game to victory.', 'study_insight': '15 = (3 × 4) + 3. Removing the remainder of 3 secures the winning state.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Digit Difference', 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Magic 9 Subtraction Mystery and Reversal Logic', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Pick any two different digits, say <strong>7</strong> and <strong>2</strong>. What happens when you explore their difference? Try this fascinating numerical trick:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3 space-y-1 text-xs">\n                <p class="text-slate-300">1. Form the two 2-digit numbers: <strong>72</strong> and <strong>27</strong>.</p>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">2. Subtract smaller from larger: 72 − 27 = <strong>45</strong>.</p>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">3. Now take the digits of 45 (4 and 5), form 54 and 45: 54 − 45 = <strong>9</strong>!</p>\n                <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold">Try another pair: Digits 8 and 3 → 83 − 38 = 45 → 54 − 45 = 9!</p>\n                <p class="text-sky-400 font-bold">Digits 9 and 1 → 91 − 19 = 72 → 72 − 27 = 45 → 54 − 45 = 9!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Why does this always yield a multiple of 9?</strong><br>\n                Any two-digit number with digits <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> has value: 10<em>a</em> + <em>b</em>.<br>\n                Reversing the digits gives: 10<em>b</em> + <em>a</em>.<br>\n                Subtracting: (10<em>a</em> + <em>b</em>) − (10<em>b</em> + <em>a</em>) = 9<em>a</em> − 9<em>b</em> = <strong>9 × (a − b)</strong>!<br>\n                The difference is <em>always a multiple of 9</em>, which inexorably collapses down to 9!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us explore a delightful piece of number magic discovered by Indian mathematicians, including the legendary D. R. Kaprekar! Think of any two different digits, say eight and three. Use them to form a two-digit number: eighty-three. Now, reverse the digits to form thirty-eight. Next, subtract the smaller number from the larger number: eighty-three minus thirty-eight equals forty-five. Now examine forty-five: forty-five is a multiple of nine, because nine multiplied by five is forty-five! What is truly extraordinary is that the difference between the original digits, eight minus three, is five, and the answer is nine multiplied by five! Try it with any two digits you choose, like nine and two. Ninety-two minus twenty-nine is sixty-three, which is nine multiplied by seven, and nine minus two is seven! Why does this always work? Algebra gives us the beautiful proof: any two-digit number with tens digit a and ones digit b has the value ten a plus b. Its reverse is ten b plus a. When you subtract them, ten a minus a is nine a, and b minus ten b is minus nine b. This simplifies to nine times the quantity a minus b! It must always be a multiple of nine! Test different digits on the interactive engine and watch the magic of nine unfold!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Pick digits 6 and 2. What is 62 minus 26?', 'options': ['26', '36', '46', '56'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '62 − 26 = 36. Notice that 36 is 9 × 4, which is 9 × (6 − 2)!', 'study_insight': 'The difference between any 2-digit number and its reverse is always 9 times the difference between its digits: 9 × (6 − 2) = 36.'}, {'question': 'If you reverse the digits of 36 to get 63, what is 63 − 36?', 'options': ['18', '27', '9', '36'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '63 − 36 = 27. Reversing 27 gives 72, and 72 − 27 = 45, and 54 − 45 = 9!', 'study_insight': 'Repeated subtraction of reversed numbers always descends along multiples of 9 until it reaches 9.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': "King's Horses", 'subtitle': 'Pastime Math: The Stable Trick Puzzle and Corner-Sharing Logic', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">A classic recreational math story tells of a king who had a square stable with 9 rooms (a 3×3 grid). The king kept horses in the outer 8 rooms, leaving the center room empty. He checked that <strong>each of the 4 outer sides contained exactly 5 horses</strong>, believing 4 × 5 = 20 horses were safely in the stable.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Original Stable (20 Horses)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 font-mono">\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Top = 5<br>\n                        5 &nbsp; [ ] 5 &nbsp;→ Middle sides = 5 each<br>\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Bottom = 5<br>\n                        Total horses = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = <strong>20 horses</strong>.\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Caretaker Steals 2 Horses (18 Horses)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 font-mono">\n                        1 &nbsp; 3 &nbsp; 1 &nbsp;→ Top = 1+3+1 = 5<br>\n                        4 &nbsp; [ ] 4 &nbsp;→ Left/Right = 1+4+0 = 5<br>\n                        0 &nbsp; 5 &nbsp; 0 &nbsp;→ Bottom = 0+5+0 = 5<br>\n                        Total horses = 2 corners + 16 middles = <strong>18 horses</strong>!\n                    </p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200">\n                <strong>The Secret Corner-Sharing Formula:</strong><br>\n                Let <em>C</em> be the total horses placed in the 4 corners.<br>\n                Since each corner horse is shared by two sides, it is counted twice:<br>\n                <span class="font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold">Total Horses in Stable = 20 − C</span>.<br>\n                • When C = 0 (no corner horses), Total = 20.<br>\n                • When C = 1 (1 horse in a corner), Total = 19 (1 horse stolen!).<br>\n                • When C = 2 (2 horses in corners), Total = 18 (2 horses stolen!).<br>\n                On every count, each side still equals 5!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': "Our final journey puzzle is the legendary King's Horses Stable Trick! A king kept twenty fine horses in a square stable arranged in a three-by-three grid with an open courtyard in the middle. The king was very particular: every evening, he inspected the stable by counting the horses along each of the four outer sides, making sure that each side had exactly five horses! One day, a clever caretaker secretly sold two horses, leaving only eighteen horses. Yet, when the king counted all four sides that evening, he still counted five horses on every single side, without suspecting a thing! How did the caretaker pull off this trick? By using the mathematics of corner sharing! When a horse is placed in a corner room, it touches two different sides of the stable, so it is counted twice! If there are five horses in each side, the king calculates four times five, which is twenty. But the actual total number of horses is twenty minus C, where C is the number of horses placed in the four corners! By placing one horse in each of two corners, and adjusting the middle rooms, the caretaker made eighteen horses sum to five on all four sides! Explore the three stable configurations on your screen, verify each side sum, and discover the mathematical secret of corner sharing!", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In a square 3×3 stable, why does placing horses in the corner rooms allow fewer total horses to produce the same side totals?', 'options': ['Corner rooms have mirrors that duplicate horses', 'Each corner room is counted as part of two adjacent sides simultaneously', 'Corner horses eat less hay', 'Horses in middle rooms run away at night'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'A corner room touches two sides. Placing horses in corners allows them to be counted twice in the side sums, reducing the total horses needed to satisfy the side total.', 'study_insight': 'In geometry and graph theory, vertices shared by two edges contribute to both boundary constraints.'}, {'question': 'If the 4 sides of a square stable each sum to 5 horses, and the 4 corners contain a total of 2 horses, what is the actual total number of horses in the stable?', 'options': ['20 horses', '19 horses', '18 horses (20 − 2 = 18)', '16 horses'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'Total horses = (4 sides × 5) − (horses in corners counted twice) = 20 − 2 = 18 horses.', 'study_insight': 'Formula: Total Horses = 20 − C, where C is the number of horses in the corners.'}]}]
